 for  example as is suggested in some of the  stories that the the us were pivoting  and saying that there should be a regime  change in russia and which the white  house have come out afterwards and you  can see it in those stories the line  from the white house is that that's not  what president biden was calling for  that's not what he meant when he said it  so it sounded very much like he was  calling for a regime change the pr  vladimir putin should not be in charge  but the white house have walked this  back slightly and we can also see that  reaction from the kremlin where they've  hit back again and because  we see this war of words and it's  another ramping up of that between uh  nato allies and russia again and but  despite the speech being powerful there  was a lot of language in there he  referred to  putin as a butcher and there's also been  some unhappiness and i've read some of  the reaction from ukrainian politicians  who felt actually there was a lot of  words there was a lot of warm words and  harsh language in it but actually there  was very little promise to them so they  want to win this war they don't just  want it to end they want to win this war  and they felt that there was very little  in there pledges made by biden for  weapons for example and what more the  u.s could do and i think they'd hoped  that this speech at the end of his visit  to poland would really be able to kind  of  push forward and see what the u.s were  going to do how the u.s are going to  help ukraine in this um pivotal moment  because it seems like the war is going  to stretch out even longer than anybody  could have predicted and they were  looking for a little bit more so i've  been reading some of that reaction as  well which isn't really in the papers  i've seen but i think it's important to  reflect back on that yeah you make a  very good point uh the front of the  observer also quoting uh uh biden saying  uh butcher putin cannot be allowed to  stay in power what do you think about  that christina i mean ukrainians need  more than just rhetoric don't they  yes the ukrainians do need more than  rhetoric but of course the  horrible  dilemma that any country faces is  putin's threat  of escalation and that's what the holy  ghost is trying to avoid at the moment  and it's agonizing i think it's  agonizing for anybody watching what's  happening in ukraine and seeing the  atrocities that are being carried out  there on a daily basis which does look  very much like genocide and very very  clearly war crimes but it remains the  case that if we put troops if the west  puts troops into ukraine and or even  supplies uh  air cover um  then that is  that will be seen as  joining the war and then you are talking  world war iii so it is a really  agonizing situation and i can see that  biden is in a very difficult situation i  think part of what we saw in that speech  earlier was the kind of passion that he  used to show when he talked about  apartheid for example that campaigning  politician that passion  is coming out and in a sense maybe it is  misplaced because to be seen to be  calling for regime change i mean in this  mad world where anybody saying thinks of  course russia needs a different regime  of course it needs a different leader  because  putin is leading russia into disaster  it's leading into it into pariah  state status it's leading it into  economic nightmares and it's using its  young men  many of them barely children  as cannon fodder and many many many  russian mothers have already we don't  know if it's 10 000 or 15 000 but by the  time this thing ends it will be many  many more the sacrifices for apparently  nothing are absolutely  enormous but to be seen to be calling  for regime change  of course plays into the kremlin's  playbook as well because that's exactly  their point that the west is anti-russia  and it feeds into their narratives and  their propaganda so i think one could  argue that even though no sane person  would think it that putin should stay  one could argue that it is actually a  gaffe on putin's part on  joe biden's part to say this yeah i mean  this is rather strange it was clearly a  scripted speech he was reading it from  auto queue um it obviously all been  approved and then and then the white  house have to roll back slightly on on  those comments um let's leave that there  and move on to mata's um back home front  of the sunday  express rachel solve the cost of living  crisis or you'll lose  the election the fallout from the the  mini budget still on many of the uh  front pages  yes so the sunday express they've got a  poll that they've done and it's found  that 63 of people don't think that the  chancellor went far enough with his  spring statement mini budget as you say  and there's a lot of criticism of rishi  sunak after this announcement and what  the government and when the chancellor  was delivering this announcement making  a speech in the commons he had these big  pledges or what he thought were big  pledges pulling the rabbit out of the  hat that would be widely welcomed so for  instance they cut to income tax and the  rise raising the threshold for national  insurance contributions these were  things that got him a lot of cheers in  the commons but actually the whole point  of this was that we are going through a  cost of living crisis now there are  families who desperately need help now  and neither of these kick in immediately  so the income tax  cuts they won't kick in until 2024 by  2024 the national insurance contribution  threshold rise isn't until july so  that's three months of this the rise in  national insurance contributions people  will have to pay before it's then offset  so i think there's been a lot of  criticism there that he didn't go far  enough there was nothing in there to  support people in universal credit  either so there's been this criticism  and i think this poll just really shows  how widespread that feeling that the  government didn't do enough and aren't  doing enough despite this crisis that  has gone on with so many families up and  down the country impacted by rising  costs of energy bills fuel and what they  buy in the shops prices are going up and  this really just puts that in there it's  also got a figure that's 67 percent of  people um are struggling with heat and  energy costs at the moment so it shows  that this is such a massive issue and it  feels like that the chancellor took a  bit of a wrong turn during the week he  didn't do what was being asked of him  yeah christine is it fair do you think  do you think the british public do put  all of this on the chancellor's  shoulders given that he was dealt a very  difficult hand wasn't he you know  inflation going up the pandemic the  energy crisis the ukraine war  all issues that weren't of his making  well what was what was of his making was  the statement he made and the measures  he's announced which are absolutely  pathetic i think we saw rishi sunak's  true colours this week actually he  managed to present himself during the  early stages of the pandemic as mr nice  guy with his furlough measures and so on  and he managed to project himself as a  kind of compassionate nice person and  what we've seen this week is  breathtaking cynicism cynicism one might  almost say johnsonian cynicism because  this statement was designed for one  group well two groups actually tory  backbenchers and tory party members with  an eye on  his leadership chances and it was not  designed for the public good or for the  good of the british people let's be  clear this is going to plunge people  into poverty i mean to call it the cost  of living crisis is actually kind of  euphemistic it really really underplays  a scale of the problem i opened a batch  of bills the other day and thought oh my  god this is a nightmare you know i will  be okay but there are many many people  in this country who won't be okay and it  really will be a choice between heating  and eating and that's become a bit of a  cliche but the escape we've heard  reports this week and people talking  people with three jobs who said they're  having to choose between having the  heating on and feeding their children i  mean this is kind of the kind of poverty  we haven't seen in this country for a  long time i think when it really hits  people are going to be desperately  shocked and upset and what what rishi  sooner offered this week was was  absolutely insulting to the british  people of course he could have done more  he was clinging on to his thatcherite  ideological small state view and it's  just not up to the challenge okay uh  matt's cartoon in the telegraph  uh so good as always price promise  everything cheaper than it will be very  soon uh he says to a  shopper there rachel just in one  sentence is that fair what christina has  said that this this mini budget wasn't  designed for the good of the british  people  i think there's a real feeling that this  budget doesn't do what it was what  people wanted it to do and i think  there's a lot of frustration  out there and we've seen that you know  he made the speech and it was going down  well as christina said with toy mps in  the chamber but as soon as that speech  was finished there was um you know a lot  of concern a lot of frustration from  various people that the government need  to look at this again and come back with  more okay all right we will leave that  there thank you both very much coming up  the duke of cambridge's surprising  statement on the final day of his  caribbean tour discussing that in a  couple of minutes  [Music]  welcome back part two of the press  preview christina and rachel still with  us uh rachel front page of the observer  international nurses in the uk are  forced to pay up if they quit explain  this to us  so this story is as you say about  international nurses who have been hired  from abroad  and in their contract there is a  stipulation that if they leave this job  and they they want to move to something  else or return home they will have to  pay back  money that was used in that hiring  process so  for things like um  travel for example and other things  around their hiring process they've been  asked to pay that back and there's a  figure in there of up to fourteen  thousand pounds that nurses are  facing and if you leave i think i found  the story um quite shocking actually  it's understandable why um  the comments from president biden are  the splash on the paper so it's on the  front page which is great and i find  that the detail in this particularly  shocking um around what you know these  people have come to the uk to do these  jobs work for nhs trusts it's not just  private companies they're talking here  and they're being given these really  quite um harsh demands that should they  leave and within a time frame they will  have to pay this back and that's an  investigation that's been done by the  observer and something they've been  looking into  yeah and christina designed to to keep  staff on and and get back some of the  recruitment costs but uh you know they  could end up with huge debt  i thought it was really shocking i mean  i think in a way it's bad enough  poaching nurses from other countries  where they're often needed much more and  often where they've been trained in  their own countries and then uh and are  much needed but we lure them with what  are the probably higher wages here not  very high but probably higher than in  their home countries and then to saddle  them with all these extra costs i think  is really shocking and we mustn't forget  that during the pandemic the strain  still continues in many hospitals and  and for nurses  and doctors but  you know nurses the the stress of  working day in day out in in sort of  busy covered boards was absolutely  dreadful and many i've met nurses who've  suffered post-traumatic stress disorder  from the time they spent during the 